  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Triwest Healthcare Alliance obligated $12.6B in federal contract dollars in FY2026 to date. Triwest won 21 awards so far this fiscal year, with an average award size of $599M, and obligations are down 6.0% year over year. Triwest received the bulk of its FY2026 obligations from the Department of Veterans Affairs with $7.9B and from the Department of Defense with $4.6B. Triwest’s work sits largely in Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers, which accounted for $12.3B of obligations, while Pharmacy Benefit Management and related administration accounted for $227M. Historical obligations show growth from $6.7B in FY21 to $13.4B in FY25 before the current-year total of $12.6B. The dataset covers contract obligations except where table notes specify award-value figures as total contract values, and current-year totals are partial fiscal year to date. Use these figures to compare buying agencies, market concentration, and year-over-year movement when preparing proposals, pricing, or capture plans for Triwest opportunities.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Who are Triwest Healthcare Alliance’s largest federal buyers?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $7.9B to Triwest in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense obligated $4.6B in the same period. These two agencies account for the total FY2026 obligations reported for Triwest."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and contract sizes dominate Triwest Healthcare Alliance’s portfolio?",
        "body": "Direct Health and Medical Insurance Carriers is the largest market for Triwest with $12.3B in obligations. Pharmacy Benefit Management and Other Third Party Administration is a secondary market with $227M. Triwest’s average award size in FY2026 to date is $599M across 21 awards."
      }
    ]
  },
